CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Written Response to Reading\n\nPost to Canvas by class time on 
 Thursday\, 08/27\n\nIn their introduction to Chapter 5 titled “Literacie
 s\,” Elizabeth Wardle and Doug Downs\, the editors of Writing About Writ
 ing\, invite you to “consider how you became the literate person you are
  today” (256).\n\nChoose one of the following groups of related question
 s (adapted from ones offered in the textbook) and compose a written respon
 se (minimum 250 words). In this short reflection\, be as specific and desc
 riptive as possible. Please be sure to use complete sentences! \n\nOption
  A: Reading as a literacy practice\n\n* When and how did you learn to read
 ? Did you find it easy to read? If so\, discuss how and why might that hav
 e been.\n\n* Was learning to read challenging for you? If so\, discuss how
  and why that might have been.\n\n* Do you like to read? If so\, what kind
 (s) of things do you most like to read\, and why?\n\n* Do you dislike read
 ing? If so\, why?\n\nOption B: Writing as a literacy practice\n\n* When an
 d how did you learn to write? Was learning to write challenging for you? I
 f so\, discuss how and why that might have been the case.\n\n* Do you like
  to write? If so\, what kind(s) of writing do you most like to do\, and wh
 y?\n\n* Do you dislike writing? If so\, why?\n\nOption C: Digital literacy
  practices\n\n* What technologies (from pencils to phones to social media 
 platforms and computer software) impact your literacy practices (a.k.a. ho
 w you read and write)?\n\nOption D: Listening and Speaking as literacy pra
 ctices\n\n* What languages or different forms of the same language did you
  speak\, read\, or write at home and elsewhere? How\, if at all\, do you t
 hink this might have influenced your literacy practices?\n\nNOTE: I will b
 e grading responses to these questions based on the quality of your though
 ts and the effort you put into crafting your post\, NOT the answers you gi
 ve! If you do not like to read\, it is perfectly acceptable to say so. The
  same goes for a favorite book or favorite author. If you don't have these
 \, say so. 
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Discussion Post due Aug. 27: Literacies and How We Practice Them [E
 RH-101-22-FL26]
URL;VALUE=URI:https://vmi.instructure.com/calendar?include_contexts=course_
 25748&month=08&year=2026#assignment_296518
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Written Response to Reading </stron
 g></p>\n<p><em>Post to Canvas by class time on Thursday\, 08/27</em></p>\n
 <p>In their introduction to Chapter 5 titled “Literacies\,” Elizabeth 
 Wardle and Doug Downs\, the editors of <em>Writing About Writing</em>\, in
 vite you to “consider how you became the literate person you are today
 ” (256).</p>\n<p>Choose <strong>one</strong> of the following groups of 
 related questions (adapted from ones offered in the textbook) and compose 
 a written response (minimum 250 words). In this short reflection\, be as s
 pecific and descriptive as possible. Please be sure to use complete senten
 ces!&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><u>Option A: Reading as a literacy practice</u></p>\n<
 ul>\n<li>When and how did you learn to read? Did you find it easy to read?
  If so\, discuss how and why might that have been.</li>\n<li>Was learning 
 to read challenging for you? If so\, discuss how and why that might have b
 een.</li>\n<li>Do you like to read? If so\, what kind(s) of things do you 
 most like to read\, and why?</li>\n<li>Do you dislike reading? If so\, why
 ?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><u>Option B: Writing as a literacy practice</u></p>\n<ul
 >\n<li>When and how did you learn to write? Was learning to write challeng
 ing for you? If so\, discuss how and why that might have been the case.</l
 i>\n<li>Do you like to write? If so\, what kind(s) of writing do you most 
 like to do\, and why?</li>\n<li>Do you dislike writing? If so\, why?</li>\
 n</ul>\n<p><u>Option C: Digital literacy practices</u></p>\n<ul>\n<li>What
  technologies (from pencils to phones to social media platforms and comput
 er software) impact your literacy practices (a.k.a. how you read and write
 )?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><u>Option D: Listening and Speaking as literacy practic
 es</u></p>\n<ul>\n<li>What languages or different forms of the same langua
 ge did you speak\, read\, or write at home and elsewhere? How\, if at all\
 , do you think this might have influenced your literacy practices?</li>\n<
 /ul>\n<p><strong>NOTE:</strong> I will be grading responses to these quest
 ions based on the quality of your thoughts and the effort you put into cra
 fting your post\, NOT the answers you give! If you do not like to read\, i
 t is perfectly acceptable to say so. The same goes for a favorite book or 
 favorite author. If you don't have these\, say so.&nbsp\;</p>
END:VEVENT
