CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Based on Swales' discussion of types of discourse communities a
 nd what makes a community\, consider the role of allegiance in determining
  community membership.  To what extent is identifying with and feeling so
 me loyalty to a discourse community essential for being a part of it? Supp
 ort your response to this prompt with at least one example\, and preferabl
 y more\, from your own life. \n\nNote: There is no minimum word count for
  this assignment. However\, I expect you to develop your answer fully.
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Discussion Post due Sept. 29--Swales: Allegiance and the Discourse 
 Community [ERH-101-22-FL26]
URL;VALUE=URI:https://vmi.instructure.com/calendar?include_contexts=course_
 25748&month=09&year=2026#assignment_296522
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>Based on Swales' discussion of types of dis
 course communities and what makes a community\, consider the role of alleg
 iance in determining community membership.&nbsp\; To what extent is identi
 fying with and feeling some loyalty to a discourse community essential for
  being a part of it? Support your response to this prompt with at least on
 e example\, and preferably more\, from your own life.&nbsp\;</p>\n<p>Note:
  There is no minimum word count for this assignment. However\, I expect yo
 u to develop your answer fully.</p>
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20260802T174300Z
UID:event-assignment-296523
DTSTART:20261001T155000Z
DTEND:20261001T155000Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:First Paragraph (Min. 125 words):\n\nKlass describes entering t
 he hospital as a medical student "in a state of primeval innocence\," but 
 quickly learning all the "endless jargon and abbreviations." What she is d
 escribing is the process of enculturating into the discourse community. Ho
 w does this kind of enculturation happen in general\, and what are the con
 sequences if someone fails to enculturate in accordance with community sta
 ndards? \n\nSecond Paragraph (Min. 125 words):\n\nIn his essay "Coaches C
 an Read\, Too\," Sean Branick states\, "There have been many articles writ
 ten on the X's and O's of the game ... however\, scholars have yet to stud
 y a coach's ability to read his players and the game as a form of literacy
 ." Does Branick convince you that these abilities are\, in fact\, a form o
 f literacy? Why or why not? \n\n 
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Discussion Post due Oct. 1: Klass and Branick [ERH-101-22-FL26]
URL;VALUE=URI:https://vmi.instructure.com/calendar?include_contexts=course_
 25748&month=10&year=2026#assignment_296523
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>First Paragraph (Min. 125 words):</
 strong></p>\n<p>Klass describes entering the hospital as a medical student
  "in a state of primeval innocence\," but quickly learning all the "endles
 s jargon and abbreviations." What she is describing is the process of encu
 lturating into the discourse community. How does this kind of enculturatio
 n happen in general\, and what are the consequences if someone fails to en
 culturate in accordance with community standards?&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><strong>S
 econd Paragraph (Min. 125 words):</strong></p>\n<p><span>In his essay "Coa
 ches Can Read\, Too\," Sean Branick states\, "There have been many article
 s written on the X's and O's of the game ... however\, scholars have yet t
 o study a coach's ability to read his players and the game as a form of li
 teracy." Does Branick convince you that these abilities are\, in fact\, a 
 form of literacy? Why or why not?&nbsp\;</span></p>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>
END:VEVENT
