CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Worksheet: Analyzing a Text Rhetorically\n\n \n\nTo begin to r
 hetorically analyze your chosen “text\,” respond in as much detail as 
 possible to the questions below.\n\n \n\nPut the Name of Your “Text” 
 Here:\n \n\nPut the Author/Speaker’s Name Here:\n\n \n\n* What is this
  discourse about? (In other words\, what is the topic or subject of the di
 scourse?)\n\n \n\nWhat issues are involved?\n\n \n\nWhat values or belie
 fs may be involved?\n\n \n\n \n\n* Why is the discourse needed? (In othe
 r words\, what prompted this discourse now?)\n\n \n\n       Is the di
 scourse responding to something that has happened? If so\, what?\n\n \n\n
         Is the discourse responding to something that is about to happ
 en\, or might happen if something is not done? If so\, what?\n\n \n\n  
        Why is this the “right or opportune time to speak or write”
  (kairos)?\n\n \n\n \n\n* What is this discourse trying to accomplish?\n
 \n \n\n      What are the goals of the discourse?\n\n \n\n \n\n* Who
  is the target audience? How do you know?\n\n \n\n      What are their
  beliefs\, values\, education\, experiences? Is there a mood or feeling th
 ey might share\, either before or        after the speaker/author's di
 scourse?\n\n \n\n \n\n* What are the rhetorical constraints of the disco
 urse?\n\n \n\n     What are “all the factors in the situation” (po
 sitive or negative) “that may lead the audience to be either more or les
 s sympathetic to the discourse”?\n\n \n\nDoes the rhetor address certai
 n constraints? If so\, which one(s)? How? Does the rhetor choose not to ad
 dress certain constraints? Explain.\n\n \n\n \n\n* Read your responses a
 bove. What about the rhetorical features of this text stand out? How might
  those help you develop a meaningful essay that adds to the scholarship ab
 out this particular work?
SEQUENCE:0
