CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:In-Class Writing:  Constructive Reflection\n\nReflecting on th
 e Writing of Essay #3\n\n \n\nWriting is not simply a game of chance you 
 play with your muse.  Writing is a thinking process.  To be more specifi
 c\, it is a problem-solving process . . . Good problem-solvers--such as ma
 ster chess players\, inventors\, successful scientists\, business managers
 \, or artists--typically have a great deal of knowledge and a large repert
 ory of powerful strategies to use in attacking their problems.  Good writ
 ers are the same.  They are people who have developed better ways of atta
 cking the problem of writing.\n\n--Linda Flower\, Process\, Form\, and Sub
 stance\n\n \n\nConstructive reflection is also valuable for itself\, for 
 what it captures between and among and outside and inside the drafts: the 
 writer inventing him or herself.\n\n    --Kathleen Blake Yancey\n\n \n
 \nOne of the goals of the first-year composition sequence is “to develop
  the ability to analyze texts for their strengths and weaknesses in the pr
 esentation of ideas\, paying special attention to such rhetorical consider
 ations as audience\, occasion\, organization and style.”   Now that yo
 u have completed writing your third essay\, spend a few minutes reading th
 e various drafts\, peer consultation sheets\, and instructor comments\, ke
 eping in mind the perspectives that Flower and Yancey suggest above.  Ans
 wer the following questions in as much detail as possible\, making specifi
 c references to the essays to illustrate your observations:\n\n \n\nWhat 
 did you learn in drafting and revising Essay #3 to address certain concern
 s that readers brought to your attention?\n\n \n\nWhat areas (thesis\, or
 ganization\, development\, sentence clarity\, grammatical and mechanical c
 orrectness) reflect the most substantial changes?  How do they compare to
  the areas that you devoted the most attention to in writing either of you
 r other first two essays?  Are they similar or different?\n\n \n\nHow di
 d you respond to the concerns that readers raised in your revisions?  How
  does it compare to the way you responded to readers’ questions or conce
 rns in revising the first two essays? In the end\, how effective do you th
 ink those revisions were\, and why?\n\n \n\n \n\nWhat did you learn from
  working on this essay about yourself as a writer\, reader\, and thinker\,
  and how does it connect to what you knew about yourself after completing 
 work on the first two essays?  If you are still unsure at this point\, di
 scuss what you think might be contributing to that uncertainty.\n\n \n\n
  \n\nHow would you define the “problem” of writing this essay\, and h
 ow does it compare with the way you defined the “problem” of writing e
 ither of the other two essays? \n\n \n\n \n\nWhat do you think you need
  to know (or do) next? And why?
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Reflective Writing Assignment: Essay No. 3 [ERH-101-22-FL26]
URL;VALUE=URI:https://vmi.instructure.com/calendar?include_contexts=course_
 25748&month=11&year=2026#assignment_296563
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="text-align: center\;"><strong>In-Cla
 ss Writing:&nbsp\; </strong><strong>Constructive Reflection</strong></p>\n
 <p style="text-align: center\;"><strong><em>Reflecting on the Writing of E
 ssay #3</em></strong></p>\n<p style="text-align: center\;">&nbsp\;</p>\n<p
 >Writing is not simply a game of chance you play with your muse.&nbsp\; Wr
 iting is a thinking process.&nbsp\; To be more specific\, it is a problem-
 solving process . . . Good problem-solvers--such as master chess players\,
  inventors\, successful scientists\, business managers\, or artists--typic
 ally have a great deal of knowledge and a large repertory of powerful stra
 tegies to use in attacking their problems.&nbsp\; Good writers are the sam
 e.&nbsp\; They are people who have developed better ways of attacking the 
 problem of writing.</p>\n<p>--Linda Flower\, <em>Process\, Form\, and Subs
 tance</em></p>\n<p><strong>&nbsp\;</strong></p>\n<p>Constructive reflectio
 n is also valuable for itself\, for what it captures between and among and
  outside and inside the drafts: the writer inventing him or herself.</p>\n
 <p><em>&nbsp\;</em><em>&nbsp\; &nbsp\;--Kathleen Blake Yancey</em></p>\n<p
 >&nbsp\;</p>\n<p>One of the goals of the first-year composition sequence i
 s “to develop the ability to analyze texts for their strengths and weakn
 esses in the presentation of ideas\, paying special attention to such rhet
 orical considerations as audience\, occasion\, organization and style.”&
 nbsp\;&nbsp\; Now that you have completed writing your third essay\, spend
  a few minutes reading the various drafts\, peer consultation sheets\, and
  instructor comments\, keeping in mind the perspectives that Flower and Ya
 ncey suggest above.&nbsp\; <strong>A<em>nswer the following questions in a
 s much detail as possible\, making specific references to the essays to il
 lustrate your observations:</em></strong></p>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><strong>
 What</strong> did you learn in drafting and revising Essay #3 to address c
 ertain concerns that readers brought to your attention?</p>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p
 >\n<p><strong>What</strong> areas (thesis\, organization\, development\, s
 entence clarity\, grammatical and mechanical correctness) reflect the most
  substantial changes?&nbsp\; <strong>How do they compare</strong> to the a
 reas that you devoted the most attention to in writing either of your othe
 r first two essays?&nbsp\; Are they similar or different?</p>\n<p>&nbsp\;<
 /p>\n<p><strong>How </strong>did you respond to the concerns that readers 
 raised in your revisions?&nbsp\; <strong><em>How does it compare</em></str
 ong> to the way you responded to readers’ questions or concerns in revis
 ing the first two essays? In the end\, <strong>how</strong> effective do y
 ou think those revisions were\, and why?</p>\n<p><strong>&nbsp\;</strong><
 /p>\n<p><strong>&nbsp\;</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What</strong> did you lea
 rn from working on this essay about yourself as a writer\, reader\, and th
 inker\, and <strong>how does it connect</strong> to what you knew about yo
 urself after completing work on the first two essays?&nbsp\; If you are st
 ill unsure at this point\, discuss what you think might be contributing to
  that uncertainty.</p>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><strong>&nbsp\;</strong></p>\n<
 p><strong>How</strong> would you define the “problem” of writing this 
 essay\, and <strong>how does it compare</strong> with the way you defined 
 the “problem” of writing either of the other two essays?&nbsp\;</p>\n<
 p>&nbsp\;</p>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><strong>What</strong> do you think you n
 eed to know (or do) next? And why?</p>
END:VEVENT
